{
  "introduction": [
    "These worksheets turn the book's exercises into records you can reuse. Begin with the pages that address your current work. A practice trader may need a model card and review sheet; a trader considering an evaluation also needs an account fact sheet and a spending plan.",
    "You do not need to complete every page every day. Keep the standing documents current, use the session and trade records when relevant, and schedule the broader reviews.",
    "Complete each blank with an observable instruction, an actual figure, or a reference to a dated procedure. Mark a field “not applicable” only when you know why it does not apply. Mark unresolved information “unknown” and identify what must be verified before the related action can proceed.",
    "These are educational planning aids. Completing them does not establish an edge, certify readiness, or guarantee protection from losses. Use the exact rules and calculations applicable to your account. Do not copy a numerical example from the book as your personal risk limit.",
    "Keep original entries. Date later corrections and distinguish information recorded at the time from explanations reconstructed afterward. Preserve separate records for simulated and live activity, while linking related decisions where useful."
  ],
